Feature: Users List Pagination
  As a caller of the user service
  I want GET /users to answer a page of users at a time, ten by default
  So that a long list of users does not arrive all at once

  Background:
    Given the user service is running

  # Why: The core ask — with nothing asked for, the first page is ten users.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] A page lists users in the order they were created, oldest first
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: The first page holds ten users when no limit is given
    Given twelve users exist
    When a GET request to /users is sent with no limit parameter
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should hold the first ten users

  # Why: A caller may ask for fewer than the default.
  @key-example
  Scenario: A smaller page is returned when a limit is given
    Given twelve users exist
    When a GET request to /users is sent with limit 5
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should hold the first five users

  # Why: Paging is only paging if the second page can be reached.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] The second page is asked for with an offset parameter counted in users
  @key-example
  Scenario: The second page follows on from the first
    Given twelve users exist
    When a GET request to /users is sent with limit 10 and offset 10
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should hold the remaining two users

  # Why: Just-inside boundary — exactly the default fills one page with nothing left over.
  @boundary
  Scenario: Exactly ten users fill one page with nothing left over
    Given exactly ten users exist
    When a GET request to /users is sent with no limit parameter
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should hold all ten users

  # Why: Just-outside boundary — one more than the default spills one user onto the next page.
  @boundary
  Scenario: Eleven users leave one for the second page
    Given eleven users exist
    When a GET request to /users is sent with limit 10 and offset 10
    Then the status code should be 200
    And the response body should hold exactly one user

  # Why: A page of nothing is a mistake, and the caller should be told so.
  @negative
  Scenario: A limit of zero is refused
    Given three users exist
    When a GET request to /users is sent with limit 0
    Then the status code should be 400
    And the response body should say the limit must be a positive whole number

  # Why: The service caps a page so one request cannot pull the whole table.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] The largest limit a caller may ask for is 100
  @negative
  Scenario: A limit above one hundred is refused
